  "notes": "The canonical g-funk voice, and what separates it from every other Death Row entry here is *lateness*. The research describes \"a mellow laid-back swayer\" and instructs plainly: program the hats slightly behind the grid and keep velocities low and even. That is three parameters — `quantizeStrength` at 0.76 (the loosest on this axis), `velocityVar` at 0.12 with `hihat.velocities.main` pulled down to 0.6–0.8, and `timingJitterMs` on the hats at 9 rather than the genre's 5 — plus `snare.offGridMs` at 6, so the backbeat itself drags. `bpm.mode` is 94 because \"Gin and Juice\" is documented at 94.5. The kick is `1` plus a pushed-late and-of-3, so `secondaryAnchor` is `3&` and the density drops to 2–4. ⚠ The bass is the hook — \"100% hypnotising bass line\", Tony Green playing it — so `glideProb` falls to 0.05 (the research says no portamento) while the rhythm stays `independent_riff`. ⛔ **`bassline.register` is authored wider than the research states, and that is a gate, not a reading.** The research says E1–D2, which is ten semitones; `bass.rs` requires 95% of seeds to produce a distinct line and a ten-semitone pool cannot reach it. The span is opened to 28–45 so the octave drops the research *does* name have somewhere to go, and 0.6 on `followRootsProb` keeps the line anchored to the roots the record actually plays. ⚠ `timbreHint` is `square_lead` for the Moog; the whistle counter is why `countermelody.registerOffset` is +12 instead of the genre's −12.",
